  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Gavin Sheets went 3-for-3 with two home runs and drove in four runs as the Padres extended their winning streak to three games with an 8-3 win over Seattle at T-Mobile Park in a day game. Lucas Giolito managed five innings, allowing just one hit and three runs while striking out three. The bullpen was pristine. Yuki Matsui and Bradgley Rodriguez combined for four scoreless innings with five strikeouts. The Padres struck first in the first inning and never trailed, controlling the game from the opening pitch. Ten hits and a 5-run sixth inning put the game out of reach. The team converted five of twelve runners left in scoring position.",
    "what_this_means": "Sheets supplied the power, but this was a team offense with multiple contributors. Ty France went 3-for-4 with three RBI, and the lineup sustained pressure throughout, working twelve RISP situations and cashing in when it mattered. Particularly in the sixth inning when five runs crossed. The real story is that the Padres' pitching set them up early and the offense actually finished the job, a combination this division race demands. Giolito's efficiency and the bullpen's shutdown work left no room for late drama. That matters because these three wins have all come by different margins and different routes. The team is learning to win in multiple ways. Three straight wins without a single wasted at-bat.",
    "what_to_watch": "Yoshinobu Yamamoto and the Dodgers are up tonight at 6:40 PM PT, with Michael King taking the ball for San Diego. King has posted a 3.10 ERA over his last four starts and faces a Dodgers lineup that's yet to truly break out this season. An opening for the Padres to keep momentum if the offense stays sharp.",
